---
import BaseLayout from "../../layouts/BaseLayout.astro";
import Orrery from "../../components/Orrery.astro";
import Timeline from "../../components/Timeline.astro";

const base = import.meta.env.BASE_URL.replace(/\/$/, "");
---

<BaseLayout
  title="About Me — Allegra Farrar"
  description="Graduate researcher at MIT AeroAstro and the Media Lab, working on adaptive sensing for Earth observation, extreme weather, and community resilience."
>
  <!-- ============================ BIO ============================ -->
  <section class="bio">
    <div class="wrap">
      <div class="bio-grid">
        <div>
          <span class="eyebrow">who I am</span>
          <h1>Hi, I'm <span class="grad-text">Allegra.</span></h1>
          <p class="lead mt-16">
            I'm a graduate researcher at MIT, working jointly in the Media Lab and
            the Department of Aeronautics and Astronautics with the
            <strong>Space Enabled Research Group</strong>. I received my Bachelor of
            Science in Mechanical Engineering from George Washington University, and
            completed my master's thesis at MIT AeroAstro designing trajectory
            optimization approaches that managed the uncertainty of a crewed Mars
            lander.
          </p>
          <p class="mt-16">
            My current research is shaped by the urgent need to address the
            inequities faced by coastal communities, where the impacts of tropical
            cyclones are amplified by limited sensing infrastructure and growing
            climate uncertainty. In response, I'm reimagining the design of Earth
            observation satellite missions for more timely, relevant, and actionable
            observations.
          </p>
          <p class="mt-16">
            My thesis blends my background in autonomous systems, orbital mechanics,
            and Earth science to develop an adaptive sensing framework for
            Earth-observing satellites, combining probabilistic machine learning
            tools with community-informed design for real-time decision systems. My
            goal is to construct observation methods that serve both scientific
            inquiry and community resilience to natural hazards.
          </p>

          <div class="flex gap-16 mt-24" style="flex-wrap: wrap;">
            <a class="btn frizzle-cursor" href={`${base}/cv/`}>Full CV &rarr;</a>
            <a class="btn frizzle-cursor" href="mailto:adfarrar@mit.edu">adfarrar@mit.edu</a>
          </div>
        </div>

        <div class="bio-photo">
          <img
            src={`${base}/images/profile.jpg`}
            alt="Allegra Farrar"
            width="520"
            height="520"
            loading="eager"
          />
          <span class="photo-ring" aria-hidden="true"></span>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <!-- ======================= INTERACTIVE ORRERY ======================= -->
  <section class="reveal">
    <div class="wrap">
      <span class="eyebrow">take the scenic route</span>
      <h2>Four places worth clicking</h2>
      <p class="lead">
        Earth, Mars, the Moon, and the Sun–Earth L2 point — each one is a piece of
        how I got here. Click any of them.
      </p>
      <div class="mt-24">
        <Orrery />
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <!-- ======================== RESEARCH INTERESTS ====================== -->
  <section class="reveal">
    <div class="wrap">
      <span class="eyebrow">what I work on</span>
      <h2>Extreme weather, earlier alerts, usable data</h2>
      <p class="lead">
        [Placeholder — refine this language later.] The through-line across
        everything I do: shorten the gap between when a hazard becomes detectable
        and when someone on the ground can act on it.
      </p>

      <div class="grid cols-3 mt-40">
        <article class="card">
          <span class="card-num">01</span>
          <h3>Extreme weather &amp; environmental hazards</h3>
          <p>
            Tropical cyclones first — cyclogenesis, rapid intensification, and the
            near-surface wind fields that remain one of the hardest problems in
            operational meteorology. The same framing extends to coastal flooding,
            landslides, and drought.
          </p>
        </article>
        <article class="card">
          <span class="card-num">02</span>
          <h3>Early alert systems</h3>
          <p>
            Detection is only useful if it arrives inside a decision window. I work
            on the onboard side of that problem: what a satellite can notice, decide,
            and flag before a storm is officially a storm.
          </p>
        </article>
        <article class="card">
          <span class="card-num">03</span>
          <h3>Timely, usable data for regional decision-makers</h3>
          <p>
            Working directly with the people who issue warnings and position
            equipment — CARICOOS, the NWS San Juan office, Landslide Ready — so the
            metrics the system optimizes are the ones they actually use.
          </p>
        </article>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <!-- ============================ MOTIVATION ========================== -->
  <section class="reveal">
    <div class="wrap">
      <span class="eyebrow">why this work</span>
      <h2>Two things put me here</h2>

      <div class="grid cols-2 mt-24">
        <article class="card motive">
          <span class="motive-tag" style="color: var(--coral); border-color: rgba(250,90,103,0.4);">
            the reason
          </span>
          <h3>Hurricane Katrina, and <em>When the Levees Broke</em></h3>
          <p>
            [Placeholder — your words to come.] The devastation of Hurricane Katrina,
            and Spike Lee's documentary <em>When the Levees Broke: A Requiem in Four
            Acts</em>, are why I care about this problem specifically. Katrina made
            unmistakable that a hazard's damage is not distributed by meteorology
            alone — it follows where infrastructure, information, and response
            capacity were already thin.
          </p>
          <p>
            [Placeholder: add what watching it meant to you, and how it reframed what
            you thought engineering was for.]
          </p>
        </article>

        <article class="card motive">
          <span class="motive-tag" style="color: var(--sand); border-color: rgba(249,208,149,0.4);">
            the method
          </span>
          <h3>Mars taught me how to act under uncertainty</h3>
          <p>
            [Placeholder — refine.] My master's work put a crewed-scale vehicle
            through Mars entry using an iterative LQR approach, choosing control
            actions that stay safe across a wide range of atmospheric conditions that
            cannot be known at time of flight. The atmosphere was the uncertain
            thing, and the job was to decide well anyway.
          </p>
          <p>
            Swapping planets does not change that structure. On Earth I have more
            data and far more people depending on the answer — but it is still a
            question of choosing the next action while the atmosphere refuses to
            resolve.
          </p>
        </article>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <!-- ============================= TIMELINE =========================== -->
  <section class="reveal">
    <div class="wrap">
      <span class="eyebrow">the road so far</span>
      <h2>Research timeline</h2>
      <p class="lead">
        [Placeholder — you'll fill in the detail per entry; the dates are from your CV.]
      </p>
      <div class="card mt-24">
        <Timeline />
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <!-- ========================= BEYOND THE THESIS ====================== -->
  <section class="reveal">
    <div class="wrap">
      <span class="eyebrow">beyond the thesis</span>
      <h2>Where this framework goes next</h2>
      <p class="lead">
        [Placeholder — expand later.] Adaptive sensing is not really about
        hurricanes. It is about any hazard where the useful observation is rare,
        time-critical, and currently thrown away. A few directions I want to push it.
      </p>

      <div class="grid cols-2 mt-24">
        <article class="card">
          <h3>Urban heat mapping</h3>
          <p>
            [Placeholder.] Heat is the deadliest hazard in this list and the most
            unevenly distributed within a single city. Targeted thermal observation
            at neighbourhood scale, triggered by conditions rather than schedule.
          </p>
        </article>
        <article class="card">
          <h3>Coastal planning &amp; erosion</h3>
          <p>
            [Placeholder.] Longer-horizon decisions — where to build, defend, or
            retreat — need change detection at a cadence that current tasking
            doesn't prioritise.
          </p>
        </article>
        <article class="card">
          <h3>Water security &amp; drought</h3>
          <p>
            [Placeholder — the Angola Global Classroom work fits here.] Mapping
            aquifers and surface-water change to support local supply decisions in
            drought-affected regions.
          </p>
        </article>
        <article class="card">
          <h3>Landslide &amp; post-storm response</h3>
          <p>
            [Placeholder.] The window right after a storm, when emergency managers
            are assessing what moved and what is about to. Different urgency, same
            architecture.
          </p>
        </article>
      </div>

      <div class="card center closing mt-40">
        <span class="eyebrow center">say hello</span>
        <h2>Questions, collaborations, or hurricane-season commiseration</h2>
        <p class="lead center" style="margin-inline: auto;">
          Find me in the Media Lab or AeroAstro, or read the project outline for the
          technical detail.
        </p>
        <div class="flex gap-16 mt-24" style="justify-content: center; flex-wrap: wrap;">
          <a class="btn primary frizzle-cursor" href={`${base}/project/`}>
            The project outline &rarr;
          </a>
          <a class="btn frizzle-cursor" href="mailto:adfarrar@mit.edu">Email me</a>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>
</BaseLayout>

<style>
  .bio { padding-top: clamp(28px, 4.5vh, 56px); }

  .bio-grid {
    display: grid;
    grid-template-columns: 1.15fr 0.85fr;
    align-items: center;
    gap: clamp(26px, 4.5vw, 60px);
  }

  .bio-photo { position: relative; justify-self: center; }

  .bio-photo img {
    width: 100%;
    max-width: 360px;
    height: auto;
    aspect-ratio: 1 / 1;
    object-fit: cover;
    border-radius: 22px;
    border: 1px solid var(--card-bd);
    box-shadow: 0 28px 66px -24px rgba(0, 0, 0, 0.75);
    position: relative;
    z-index: 1;
  }

  /* soft accent halo behind the headshot */
  .photo-ring {
    position: absolute;
    inset: -8%;
    border-radius: 50%;
    background: radial-gradient(circle, rgba(80, 180, 149, 0.2), rgba(80, 180, 149, 0) 68%);
    filter: blur(20px);
    z-index: 0;
    max-width: 100%;
  }

  @media (max-width: 900px) {
    .bio-grid { grid-template-columns: 1fr; }
    .bio-photo { order: -1; }
  }

  .card-num {
    display: block;
    font-family: var(--mono);
    font-size: 11px;
    letter-spacing: 0.16em;
    color: var(--blue);
    margin-bottom: 9px;
  }

  .motive { display: flex; flex-direction: column; }

  .motive-tag {
    align-self: flex-start;
    font-family: var(--mono);
    font-size: 10.5px;
    letter-spacing: 0.14em;
    text-transform: uppercase;
    padding: 3px 10px;
    border: 1px solid var(--card-bd);
    border-radius: 100px;
    margin-bottom: 12px;
  }

  .closing { padding: clamp(30px, 4vw, 46px) clamp(22px, 3vw, 38px); }
  .closing h2 { margin-inline: auto; max-width: 26ch; }
</style>
